{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1782265999,"nonce":"5tloPCkhBMJ+lvy3j4DH10YlQJ8=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABHThYCA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/167#issuecomment-4785199112"},"message":"## Validated by the first real full-charge → BMS-cutoff (2026-06-23)\n\nThe 2026-06-23 Massabesic survey ([#313](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/313)) ran the pack all the way to the BMS hard-cutoff on the water (the 2026-05-22 drain-to-LVD stopped at 21.2 V). Re-running the established coulomb method (`2026-05_charge_cycle_coulomb.py`) on a `bag_to_sqlite` DB for today, alongside the prior deployments:\n\n| Deploy | in-water h | Ah (linear) | **Ah (quad)** | % of 273 Ah | nm | Ah/nm |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 04-27 (ballast) | 5.12 | 100 | 66 | 24% | — | — |\n| 05-01 (ballast) | 4.77 | 153 | 118 | 43% | 5.8 | 20.3 |\n| 05-22 (ballast, drain test) | 2.72 | 108 | 90 | 33% | 4.9 | 18.3 |\n| 06-22 (no ballast) | 6.54 | 327 | 269 | 98% | 20.3 | 13.2 |\n| **06-23 (no ballast, ran dry)** | 6.16 | 323 | **273** | **100%** | 19.2 | 14.2 |\n\n### Conclusions\n\n1. **The quadratic PWM-coulomb model is validated** — it predicted **272.6 Ah** and the pack (273 Ah nameplate) actually emptied. Near-exact. **Use quadratic;** linear over-predicts ~19 %.\n2. **The V-drop energy model under-counts badly** — the 06-22 V-drop figure (\"~4154 Wh ≈ 59 % used\") is contradicted by the validated coulomb-quad (**~98 %**). Trust V-drop for trends, not absolute energy.\n3. **Real survey endurance ≈ 6 h / one full pack** (~42 A average). The \"8–12 h cruise\" extrapolation was optimistic. **06-22 and 06-23 both ran the pack ~dry — two consecutive days at the edge.**\n4. **Usable capacity to the BMS cutoff ≈ full nameplate (~273 Ah)**, cutoff ~20.6 V loaded.\n\n### Ballast (removed 2026-06-19; 06-22/06-23 are no-ballast)\n\n- **Specific energy (Ah/nm) does NOT show a no-ballast penalty** (13–14 vs 18–20 with ballast) — but this is **confounded by mission type** (ballast days were turn-heavy near-pier; no-ballast were real surveys), so it neither proves nor rules out a penalty.\n- **IMU pitch shows a consistent ~1.3° trim difference** (no-ballast sits more bow-down, tentatively — sign calibrated weakly from gentle survey accels), directionally consistent with the bow-plow concern, but **not convertible into a demonstrated energy penalty** given the confounds (mission type, added hydro payload, per-deployment AHRS bias).\n- **Verdict: inconclusive.** Needs a controlled matched-speed run with/without ballast (→ #88), ideally to higher speeds where plowing would bite.\n\nArtifacts: `~/data/logs/analysis/2026-06-23/` + reusable `2026-06-23_deployment.db`. Doc updates in `bizzyboat_power.md` (PR for #313/#315). The turn-back/point-of-no-return finding is [#315](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/315).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}